The present study introduces a generated,
 repeated N-of-1 trial that compares the effect of static and dynamic type systems executed on 22 participants (professional developers and PhD students).
 The code of 15 generated classes was shown to participants,
 each class consisting of a constructor and one single (empty) method.
 The participant's task was to enter a constructor call to one specific class (with a varying number of parameters).
 For the static type system,
 each parameter had an annotated (i.e.,
 written) static type,
 in the dynamic type system no such type name appeared.
 It turned out that the effect of the type system was strong and large (p < .001
\series bold
,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset


\series default
 = .163):
 Using the dynamic type system required the participants on average 121% more time (
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{M_{dynamic}}{M_{static}}$
\end_inset

 = 2.21).
 This ratio was influenced by the number of parameters.
\end_layout

