\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Knowing the types of a parameter could either help to extract an element from a given context where,
 for example,
 corresponding variables are possibly directly available.
 The type information might also help to construct matching parameters,
 either by finding the class 
\family typewriter
Point
\family default
 in the code,
 or by finding other classes that implement or extend 
\family typewriter
Point
\family default
.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under the assumption that nominal types are used for the static type system (which is the assumption of the present study and which matches languages such as Java,
 C#,
 etc.) the static type system requires for each parameter that it must be of type 
\family typewriter
Point
\family default
.
 Such constraint is not given in dynamically typed language,
 where passed parameters have to implement a method 
\family typewriter
get_Y()
\family default
.
 Hence,
 a dynamic type system can be considered as more flexible,
 because one can pass all objects that provide a method 
\family typewriter
get_Y()
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is another difference from the usability perspective.
 In programming languages such as Java successful static type checking is necessary for compiling and running code.
 In case of a type error,
 developers are informed before program execution.
 In dynamically typed languages,
 code is executed until the system reaches a non-executable piece of code.
 As a consequence,
 not only the kind of error is different between static and dynamic type systems,
 but also how developers can interact with the system:
 developers in dynamically typed languages can pass a parameter to a method in order to get feedback from the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It should be explicitly said that this possible documentation effect of static type systems (with type annotations) appears at multiple situations in the code – possibly with different effects.
 The previous argumentation focused on parameters.
 However,
 languages such as Java require to declare return types as well that also has a possible documentation effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 there are tooling aspects that probably play a role.
 For example,
 completion tools are typically better in statically typed languages.
 Such tools can also be considered as support for documentation,
 because they permit to explore APIs.
 However,
 again,
 dynamic type systems also permit to explore code (in a different way) by passing (intentionally) invalid parameters in order to get feedback at run time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Research Questions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Research-Questions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The present work focuses on two research questions:
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Research question 1:
 
\series default
Do type annotations as they appear in statically typed programming languages have a benefit in comparison to the absence of such annotations (as it typically appears in dynamically typed programming languages)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Research question 2:
 
\series default
Is it possible to design an experiment on the possible effect of type systems (with the focus on type annotations) that does not require much effort from participants and that can be easily replicated (possibly by a single person)?
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The focus of the first research question is on the technical aspect of type systems:
 the (possible) effect of type annotations on developers.
 The focus of the second one is on methodological concerns.
 
\end_layout

