\begin_layout Subsection
Initial Considerations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are a number of initial considerations that influenced the present experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Task:

\series default
 The typical task in experiments is to write code.
 The problem with it is that it probably introduces a larger deviation between (and possibly within) participants.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This argument is in line with the often-cited 10x problem,
 which argues that there is a large variation between (and possibly within) developers,
 see 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand keyonly
key "McConnell11"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 To reduce this problem,
 we decided to reduce the whole task to one single line of code:
 a constructor call.
 The parameters passed to that constructor must be gained from further constructor calls.
 An implication is that return types play a minor role,
 because the return type of a constructor corresponds to the type of its class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Type system:

\series default
 It is plausible that the kind of type system has an effect.
 We decided to use a nominal type system (i.e.,
 type checking based on type names) with type annotations (i.e.,
 where type names appear when variables or parameters are defined) without type parameters–a decision that was also done in other studies in the literature.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Subtyping:

\series default
 While a type system provides different ways to define subtype relationships,
 its effect on developers is up to our knowledge unclear.
 As a consequence,
 we decided to ignore subtyping.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Position of types:

\series default
 The position of types in source code has probably an effect on usability (see,
 for example,
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand keyonly
key "Souza_2014,FlintKDB25"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset

):
 local variables appear at different positions than fields,
 class variables,
 return types,
 etc.,
 and the position in the code probably makes a difference how developers use it.
 Hence,
 we intentionally restrict this experiment to method and constructor calls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Language:

\series default
 A programming language itself and its APIs probably influences its usability.
 Hence,
 we decided to reduce the language and the code in the experiment to a minimum:
 the language only consists of class definitions,
 constructor and method definitions,
 and constructor and method calls.
 Because of the decision to have only constructor calls in the task,
 we also decided to have constructor calls without a 
\family typewriter
new
\family default
 operator.
 Instead,
 participants just had to use the name of a class.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This choice is in line with programming languages such as Dart,
 where 
\family typewriter
new
\family default
 is optional for constructor calls,
 (see 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

https://dart.dev/language/classes#using-constructors
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Likewise Java,
 a constructor has the same name as the corresponding class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Kind of code (Part I):

\series default
 In order to remove arithmetic complexity,
 each constructor invokes one single method to each of its parameters and all methods have empty bodies.
 Due to the evidence that method names have an effect on readability (in comparison to e.g.
 abbreviations,
 see 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cite{
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand keyonly
key "HofmeisterSH19"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset

),
 we decided to use (random) readable method names from a predefined set of possible method names (such as such as 
\family typewriter
addEvents
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
connectDatabase
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
formatOutput
\family default
,
 etc.).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Kind of code (Part II):

\series default
 Concerning class names,
 we use single letter class names for the following reasons:
 While we are aware that abbreviations have a negative effect on readability,
 we also need to take into account that the experiment requires from participants to write down constructor calls,
 where writing a long name requires more time than writing down a short name.
 
\end_layout

