 that guides the participant through the experiment.
 The application contains an introduction page,
 three tutorial pages,
 two training tasks and (after the experiment) a questionnaire.
 All classes were shown on one screen arranged in a rectangle.
 The class Target,
 whose constructor should be called by a participant,
 appeared in the lower left corner,
 directly above the text field where the answer should be given (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:App"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The application gave feedback to users in a way that corresponds to typical responses in statically or dynamically typed languages,
 such as error messages 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
no such class
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 when a constructor is called,
 but the class is not contained in the code,
 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
invalid number of parameters
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 when too many or too few parameters are passed to a constructor.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If in the dynamically typed version a wrong parameter is passed,
 this means that one method call fails somewhere in the code.
 In such a case,
 an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
unknown method
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 exception is thrown with the name of the unknown method,
 the class name on which such method should be invoked,
 and the name of the constructor,
 where this wrong name appears.
 In case a wrong type is passed to a constructor in the statically typed language,
 the expected type's name is shown as well as the wrong type's name (such as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
invalid type in call.
 Expected Target(E) but got Target(F)
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in case the wrong type F is passed to Target,
 where a type E was expected).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Execution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
All participants were chosen based on purposive sampling (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "patton2014qualitative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset

We invited people we assumed to be either professional software developers or PhD students via email to participate in the experiment.
 The participants were given the link to the experiment with the additional information that the participation required up to 10 minutes.
 We only considered data from people,
 who classified themselves as developer or PhD student.
 Altogether,
 22 people participated in the experiment who matched the previous classification.
 Three additional participants (one classified himself as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

teacher
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 two classified themselves as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

other
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) were not considered for the analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Eleven participants classified themselves as professional software developer,
 eleven as PhD students.
 Nine participants classified themselves as age 25 to (excluding) 30,
 six participants responded an age between 30 and (excluding 35),
 five answered 35 to (excluding) 40,
 and two participants answered 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

40 or older
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Two participants answered that they have no experience in software industry,
 nine participants answered that they have 5 years (or more) industry experience.
 Only two participants expressed that they prefer dynamic type systems,
 three answered that they do not think that a type system matters much,
 one participant did not answer the question.
 The other 16 participants expressed a preference towards static type systems.
\end_layout

