\begin_layout Standard
All participants have a ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mathrm{M_{dynamic}}}{M_{statix}}$
\end_inset

 > 1,
 i.e.,
 all participants required more time with the dynamic type system than the static type system.
 However,
 the differences are noteworthy:
 while participant 3 required just 26% more time,
 participant 2 required 360% more time using the dynamic type system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Four of 22 participants did not reveal a significant result of the main variable type system (although one of them– participant 22–is approaching a significant effect).
 All of the four participants have one common characteristic:
 all of them were PhD students.
 Running a 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

-test reveals that the variable status has a significant effect on whether t is significant (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

(1,
 N = 22) = 4.89,
 p = .027,
 see Table 
