\begin_layout Subsection
Results per Participant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The results of the analysis on each individual participant is given in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:AnalysisPerSubject_InclStats"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:AnalysisPerSubject_InclStats"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 also includes the sum of times required by each participant.
 In contrast to all previously mentioned numbers,
 we included the outliers in order to determine how much time each participant took on the experiment tasks.
 The fastest participant (developer no 11) required 2.5 minutes,
 the slowest participant required 11.9 minutes (PhD student no 9).
 The average experiment time per participant was 4.7 minutes,
 i.e.,
 a single data point was on average selected in 23.4 seconds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Float table
placement document
alignment document
wide true
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Results per participant:
 The column b describes the background (d = developer,
 s = PhD student),
 the columns t (type system),
 p (number of parameters) and t * p (interaction between both variables) the p-values for the corresponding variable (based on an ANOVA executed on each individual participant).
 In o the number of outliers (per participant) is shown.
 The columns y (year of working experience),
 e (programming experience),
 impr (impression),
 pr (preference),
 and c (change) contain the participant's responses to the questionnaire.
 E% (effect) describes 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mathrm{M_{dynamic}}}{M_{statix}}-1$
\end_inset

 rounded to full percent,
 d the total experiment duration (in minutes,
 including outliers).
 Those participants,
 for whom the main variable type system did not show an effect,
 are highlighted.
 The participants that were considered as non-conform to the experiment procedure are marked with a question mark.
