\begin_layout Plain Layout
All participants have a ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mathrm{M_{dynamic}}}{M_{statix}}$
\end_inset

 > 1,
 i.e.,
 all participants required more time with the dynamic type system than the static type system.
 However,
 the differences are noteworthy:
 while participant 3 required just 26% more time,
 participant 2 required 360% more time using the dynamic type system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Four of 22 participants did not reveal a significant result of the main variable type system (although one of them – participant 22– is approaching a significant effect).
 All of the four participants have one common characteristic:
 all of them were PhD students.
 Running a 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

-test reveals that the variable status has a significant effect on whether or not t is significant (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

(1,
 N = 22) = 4.89,
 p = .027,
 see Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:ChiQuared_Tests_NoEffect"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

):
 PhD students are more likely to show no effect of the main variable.
 While two of those four PhD students expressed that they prefer statically typed languages,
 one stated to be indifferent with respect to the type system.
 The other PhD student expressed a preference towards dynamic type systems.
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

-test on whether the answer to the preference question has an effect on the likelihood to show no significant effect does not show a significant result (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

(3,
 N = 21) = 2.08,
 p = .353,
 see Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:ChiQuared_Tests_NoEffect"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 Furthermore,
 it turns out that the response to the question on the years of working experience (w) has an effect on whether or not the main variable shows an effect (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

(4,
 N = 22) = 10.3,
 p = .035):
 the larger the working experience,
 the lower the likelihood that the type system shows no effect.
 It is plausible that status and years of working experience are related.
 However,
 in the sample the status reveals only a result approaching significance with respect to the working experience (U = 31.5;
 p = .055).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
With respect to the variable number of parameters (p),
 two participants show no effect.
 Again,
 both participants (both,
 no 9 and no 13,
 contained in the set of participants that showed no effect of t) are PhD students.
 However,
 the background did not increase the likelihood to show an effect of p (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

(1,
 N = 22) = 2.2,
 p = .138).
 With respect to the interaction type system (t) * number of parameter (p),
 eleven participants did not reveal significant results (four developers,
 seven PhD students).
 Running again a 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

-test shows no effect of the status on the likelihood to show an interaction effect (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

(1,
 N = 22) = 7.29,
 p = .201).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
All participants except participant 18 (who did not answer the preference question) had the impression that the static type system helped in the experiment 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{21}{22}=95\%$
\end_inset

.
 I.e.,
 even those participants for whom no significant effect of type systems was detected stated that the type system helped in the experiment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
While 20 participants expressed that the experiment does not change their perspective on type systems,
 2 participants (both PhD students) agreed on that (
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{2}{22}=9\%$
\end_inset

).
 Both participants (6,
 and 16) preferred static type systems and both showed an effect of the variable type system.
\end_layout

