\begin_layout Subsection
Informal Interviews and Responses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We informally interviewed all participants (participant 3,
 9,
 15,
 16,
 17,
 and 21) whose experiment time was from our perspective longer than expected (5 minutes and more).
 More precisely,
 we asked them whether they 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

observed something unusual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 during the experiment,
 or whether they had the feeling that they 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

had troubles to do the experiment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 One participant (no 3) expressed that he did not think something unusual happened.
 Participant 21 expressed that he felt slightly unfocused during the experiment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
From four participants (9,
 15,
 16,
 and 17) we received a comparable reaction.
 All of them expressed that at a certain point in time (while doing the experiment) they had the feeling that the generated experiment code could be wrong,
 and they double checked whether the method invocations in the code were correct.
 Furthermore,
 it turned out that all outliers were contained in data sets of those participants (participant 9,
 16 and 17).
 From our perspective that could be interpreted as they did not follow the experiment protocol and instead tried to explore the experiment (instead of doing the experiment).
 All of those participants had one common characteristic:
 they were PhD students.
 I.e.,
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{4}{11}=36\%$
\end_inset

 of the PhD students we observed that they potentially behaved non-conform to the experiment procedure.
 And finally,
 it turned out that those four participants were the ones who spent the most time in the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Exploratory Analysis without Non-Conform Participants
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Taking the informal responses into account,
 we think there is a justified doubt whether four participants followed the experiment procedure and we think it is plausible to repeat the analysis without those participants (we call those four removed participants non-conform participants).
 We should point out that all identified candidates have the same status (PhD candidates),
 i.e.,
 among all six participants we interviewed the status had an effect on the suspicion that the experimental procedure was not followed (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi²$
\end_inset

(1,
 N = 6) = 6.00,
 p = .014).
 But we need to point out that the identification of those participants suffers from a potential selection bias:
 we just interviewed participants who provided from our perspective suspicious results (who spent more than five minutes on the experimental tasks).
 We cannot guarantee that there were other participants who possibly did not follow the experiment procedure.
 
\end_layout

