\begin_layout Section
Threats to Validity
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Threats-to-Validity"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are a number of threats to validity of the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Task (external threat):

\series default
 All tasks are the same–a constructor must be called.
 Obviously,
 such task is not representative for programming in general.
 Again,
 the reason for this choice was to let participants do multiple,
 comparable tasks and to remove algorithmic complexity from the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Artificial code,
 Part I (external threat)
\series default
:
 The code in the experiment is obviously artificial.
 This is not accidental.
 Instead,
 the goal was to have a highly controlled experiment.
 A typical argument against this kind of design is,
 for example,
 that the code does not do anything (because only empty methods are invoked).
 However,
 one must be aware that increasing,
 for example,
 the length of the code,
 increasing the number of applied language constructs,
 or increasing the code's complexity increases (probably) the deviation and increases the response times in general.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Artificial code,
 Part II (external threat):

\series default
 One threat that seems quite obvious is that the experiment only uses a flat structure:
 objects to be passed to the target constructor are constructed without any additional parameters.
 We do think that nested structures introduce an additional level of complexity,
 because developers need to keep in mind what class they are currently working on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Participant behavior (internal and external threat)
\series default
:
 It is quite plausible that the experiment results strongly depend on how participants do the experiment–especially for dynamic types:
 it probably takes (much longer) to read the code instead of using the browser's search function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Discussion-and-Summary"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This work was motivated by the observation that controlled type system experiments in the scientific literature hardly come up with consistent conclusions and there should be the desire to replicate at least some of the experiments.
 However,
 experiments that require up to 27 hours from a single participant (for a single data point) are too costly to replicate (which is possibly the reason why no such replications can be found)–and such experiments are too costly in order to design comparable experiments in order to study further factors that possibly influence differences between static and dynamic type system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of the present work was to build an experiment that is easy to replicate and that would still permit to study possible type system effects.
 Thereto,
 the present experiment is based on recurring speculations in the literature,
 that type names in source code have a positive documentation effect (see,
 for example,
 
