\begin_layout Standard
The present experiment was designed as an N-of-1 trial that consisted of 12 tasks.
 For each task,
 14 classes were generated,
 each consisting of one (empty) method and one constructor.
 One of these classes was the target class,
 whose constructor should be called.
 The target class constructor had a varying number of parameters (1,
 3,
 and 5),
 while the constructors of those classes whose objects had to be passed had no parameter.
 The source code for six tasks contained type information (for the static type system),
 i.e.,
 each parameter was annotated with a type name,
 while the other six tasks did not provide such type information (representing the dynamic type system).
 The participant's task was to type in a call to the target class constructor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiment was executed on 22 participants (eleven professional developers and eleven PhD students).
 On average a participant required 4.7 minutes.
 The total time for doing the experimental tasks was 1.7 hours (sum of times of all participants' experimental tasks).
 The experiment revealed an effect of the two variables (type system and number of parameters) as well as an interaction of both variables.
 I.e.,
 the experiment was able to show an effect of type systems and a variable that controls the difference between both with low costs–compared to the type system experiments in the literature.
 More precisely,
 the experiment showed that on average the dynamic type system increased the required time to create a valid constructor by 121% (p < .001
\series bold
,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset


\series default
 = .163,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{M_{dynamic}}{M_{static}}$
\end_inset

 = 2.21).
 Furthermore,
 the difference in time between the static and the dynamic type system was influenced by the number of parameters (i.e.,
 interaction between the variable type system and number of parameters,
 p < .001,
 
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset


\series default
 = .280):
 the more parameters were required by the constructor,
 the larger was the difference.
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The variable number of parameters influenced the response time as well in a strong and large way (p < .001
\series bold
,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{p}^{2}$
\end_inset


\series default
 = .323).
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to the nature of N-of-1 experiments,
 it is possible to run this experiment on one single participant.
 I.e.,
 a single person who possibly doubts the results of the experiment,
 can rerun it and analyze his data set.
 Even rerunning the whole experiment (on 22 participants) is possible with relatively few resources.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The present experiment answers from our perspective both research questions.
 First,
 it is possible to design a relatively inexpensive experiment (research question 2,
 see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Research-Questions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) that is still able to reveal strong results with respect to the effect of static type systems in comparison to dynamic type systems (research question 1,
 see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Research-Questions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we focus on the second research question and if we compare the total experiment times of the present experiment with the most expensive experiment (1,323h,
 see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Related-Work"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 respectively with the cheapest experiment (28.9h,
 see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Related-Work"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 the present experiment required only between .001% and 5.686% of the experimentation time of experiments found in literature.
 Hence,
 we think that the general approach of the experiment–designing an experiment as an N-of-1 trial based on generated tasks–permits to study factors in the field of programming in general,
 respectively type systems in particular in an inexpensive way.
 Furthermore,
 the experiment design permits a single person to participate in the experiment and to receive analyzable data (for one single person).
 As a consequence,
 a developer,
 who doubts in the results can rerun the experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Having said this,
 the experiment also revealed something else:
 four of the 22 participants did not reveal an effect of the main variable type system.
 The commonality of these participants was,
 that all of them were PhD students (all participating developers revealed a positive effect of the variable type system).
 One could speculate,
 whether PhD students are in some way a special of group participants.
 Actually,
 we did informal interviews with participants who required from our perspective surprisingly long time in the experiment.
 Feedback we gained from PhD students (but not from developers) was,
 that they had (at some point in the experiment) the feeling that the generated experiment code could be wrong,
 and they double checked whether the method invocations in the code were correct.
 We think this is an indicator that those participants did not follow the experiment procedure,
 but rather tried to find out,
 what the experiment was possibly about and whether the experimenter possibly did some faults.
 The interesting observation is,
 that this seemed to be only the case for PhD students and we think that the experiment gives a first indicator that the behavior or PhD students could be different in experiments than the behavior of professional developers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We should also point out that–while the effect of the main variable was visible to most participants–that the interaction effect was only detected on only half of the participants.
 Consequently,
 experimenters might think about how important it is that all results are visible for most participants.
 And as a reaction on this,
 experimenters might decide to increase the number of repetitions per treatment combination in the experiment–which will increase the total experimentation time per participant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With respect to the first research question,
 the present experiment gives additional information about static type systems that use type annotations.
 First,
 they do help developers.
 This statement,
 that can be already gained from experiments in the literature (see 
