\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 However,
 those experiments in the literature varied a lot in the measured times for using a static or dynamic type system (between 55% and 120%,
 see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Related-Work"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 Due to the present experiment,
 one possible explanation is found:
 Differences between times for using a static or dynamic type system depends on the the number of (type annotated) parameters in calls.
 Double-checking those experiments in the literature reveals,
 that the number of used parameters in the programming tasks was not documented.
 I.e.,
 the type system effect is not a constant – it varies under different conditions and one condition (and its effect size) was determined in the present study.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Still,
 one should not forget that the experiment is quite limited.
 First,
 it only focuses on parameters in constructor calls.
 We think that type annotations for return types have a large effect as well,
 but we also think that their effect is quite different than type annotations in parameters:
 for return types,
 people reading code without type annotations need to find out on their own where something is returned.
 Thereto,
 they possibly need to follow procedure calls,
 etc.
 Hence,
 we think that there are other factors that need to be taken into account and that need to be studied in a separate way.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 one needs to take into account that there are different kinds of type systems.
 The present paper only focuses on type systems that use type annotations.
 Actually,
 there are static type systems that do make use of type inference.
 While type inference is not overwhelmingly present in popular programming languages,
 even languages such as Java make use of type inference.
 For example,
 lambda expressions in Java do not require to declare a parameter type (whose static type is inferred and still checked).
 We also think that parameterized types also require separate studies that possibly focus not only on the name of type parameters,
 but also on upper and lower bounds (as they appear,
 for example,
 in Java as well).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiment described in the present paper suggests that the effect of static types on development time is quite large and that differences between static and dynamic types are influenced by the number of parameters (i.e.,
 the number of types for the statically typed version).
 Our interpretation is that such differences can be explained by the effort of inferring from dynamically typed code what (dynamic) types are required–effort that is not required in statically typed code with type annotations.
\end_layout

